# A small JEPA world model: the encoder knows, the predictor does not add

*Ravindu Somawansa — draft, 2026-11-02. Every number below is generated by `paper/make_paper.py` from `repro/*/expected/`; its source is in `paper/numbers.json`.*

## Abstract

We trained a small JEPA world model in the LeWorldModel recipe on teleoperated demonstrations of one tabletop task on a low-cost SO-101 arm, and re-tested on it every use of such a model that we found in recent world-model papers, each under a bar fixed in a public register. In exploratory probes on policy rollouts the model never saw in training, a linear readout of the encoder's final-frame latent separates success from failure as well as a hand-built colour judge (AUROC {{rest_W1_2}} vs {{rest_judge_2}}) and better than a frozen DINOv2 encoder, the commanded joints or the policy's identity. The predictor does not add to it: at the grasp, the imagined latent reads the outcome worse than the current one ({{c1_imagined}} vs {{c1_current}}), and every use that needs a multi-step rollout, from chunk ranking to surprise monitoring and risk prediction, misses its bar. One rig, one model, one data scale.

## Introduction

A world model in the sense of LeWorldModel (LeWM) [@maes2026lewm] has two parts: an encoder that maps camera frames to a latent vector, and a predictor that, given the current latent and a sequence of actions, imagines the latents that follow. Both are trained together from pixels with a joint-embedding predictive (JEPA) loss. Papers built on such models propose *uses* of them: ranking a policy's candidate action chunks by their imagined outcome, flagging a failure when the observed latent departs from the predicted one, reading the outcome of a grasp before it happens, planning over several time scales. Each paper reports the use that worked, on its own robot and data.

We take the opposite view of one model. We trained a single small JEPA world model on one arm and one task, and re-tested on it every use we could take from the literature. Each re-test is a row of a register: a bar written down before the result where we could, a dataset, a result and a verdict. Pre-registration here means only that: the bar and the decision rule are committed to the repository before the number exists. Audited against the commit history, all {{prereg_matched}} rows match a pre-registration record; {{bar_first}} bars were committed before their result and {{same_commit}} in the same commit as its result (the two-level hierarchical progress use). One row, the on-device latency of the pretrained RoboJEPA and V-JEPA 2.1 encoders [@zholus2026robojepa; @murlabadia2026vjepa21], is not a use of our model. Of the {{rows}} rows, {{n_pass}} pass, {{n_met}} is met as predicted, {{n_np}} are not proven, and the remaining {{n_fail}} fail or are no-go.

On the {{n}} rollouts, and in exploratory probes only, the result splits along the model. The *encoder* reads the episode's outcome from the current frame, better than a frozen foundation encoder or the robot's own joint commands, and it does so at the grasp anchor too, {{anc_lead}} frames before the gripper first closes. The *predictor*, which every forecasting use relies on, does not improve on that reading by imagining forward. The contribution is threefold: the register itself, with an audit of how every use loaded the checkpoint (Table @tab:register); exploratory controls that separate encoder from predictor (Table @tab:controls, Fig. @fig:controls); and the exploratory reading that, at this data scale, the predictor is the weak link.

**Caveats stated up front.** The encoder readouts are linear probes fit with leave-one-session-out (LOSO) cross-validation on {{n}} rollouts ({{success}} success / {{failure}} failure, {{sessions}} sessions) with operator labels. The model was trained on teleoperation only and never saw these rollouts, but the controls were chosen after the data and are **exploratory**. Among the {{n_pass}} passes, {{pass_bar_first}} have bars committed before the result, and the bar of the outcome-readout use was written retrospectively. Ordered by commit alone, without the audit, the register reads {{prereg_v1_bar_first}} bar-first and {{prereg_v1_same_commit}} same-commit.

## Setup

**Robot and data.** The robot is one SO-101 arm [@so101] driven through LeRobot [@cadene2024lerobot], with two cameras (top and wrist) and one task family: put a bowl into another bowl. The world model is trained on `ravediamond/so101_bowls_all_trimmed` ({{train_eps}} training and {{val_eps}} validation episodes, split by grid layout). The evaluation rollouts are `ravediamond/so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`, a separate dataset of SmolVLA [@shukor2025smolvla] rollouts recorded after the world model was trained.

**Model.** Our world model, named W1 in the repository, follows the LeWM recipe [@maes2026lewm]: a multi-view ViT encoder, an action-conditioned predictor and the SIGReg regulariser. It was trained on one rented GPU for ${{cost_w1}}. **Controls.** The frozen DINOv2-S control is a frozen DINOv2 small encoder [@oquab2023dinov2], mean-pooled over the top and wrist views, the design of DINO-WM [@zhou2024dinowm]. The joints control is the commanded joint vector. The colour judge is a hand-built rule on the final frame with no learning. The policy-identity control is the checkpoint that ran the episode.

**Load audit.** One early use loaded the checkpoint with `strict=False` and so ran a random encoder. We therefore re-traced the load path of every use: {{audit_trained}} ran the trained model, {{audit_random}} ran a random one (already voided) and none is unknown. The world-model block of VLA-JEPA, used in one diagnostic, loads {{wm_keys}} of {{wm_keys}} keys ({{wm_missing}} missing).

## The register

Table @tab:register lists the {{rows}} uses with what each takes from the model: the encoder alone ({{uses_encoder}}), the predictor ({{uses_predictor}}) or both ({{uses_both}}).

<!-- register -->

*Encoder reads.* The outcome readout at rest passes, with a retrospective bar; the current-latent arm of the grasp probe reads the grasp at the anchor ({{c1_current}}). The pose probes [@klindt2026lejepa] measure what else the latent holds: our encoder holds about half of the joint variance linearly (R² {{lejepa_w1}}, against {{lejepa_w2p}} for the frozen DINOv2-S control). Encoder uses that read progress or stage rather than outcome fail: progress monotonicity, two-level hierarchical progress [@zhang2026hjepa], the latent stage tracker, and SURVEYOR's latent-distance scale [@surveyor2026] (τ {{surv_tau_W1}} against {{surv_paper}} in the paper).

*Predictor forecasts.* Open-loop prediction beats copying the last latent ({{g0_err}} vs {{g0_copy}} at K = 30): the predictor does predict its own latent. Every use of that prediction fails. Chunk ranking [@maes2026lewm] reaches a top-1 accuracy of {{g1b_top1}} against a bar of {{g1b_bar}}, while the commanded actions alone reach {{g1b_actions}}. The surprise monitor catches {{w1a_hits}} failures. In the grasp probe the imagined latent ({{c1_imagined}}) reads less than the current one. The hierarchical planner [@zhang2026hwm] shows no crossover, and the conformal surprise band, the re-ranking of chunk candidates, the action-conditioned risk head [@liu2026reap] and the calibrated surprise reflex all miss their bars.

## What the encoder knows

