*Objection: the anticipation step of the outcome-readout gate is a predictive pass.* There the imagined latent beat policy identity by {{g10_anticip}}, but that bar is retrospective, and the two bar-first reruns of the same idea, the grasp probe and the calibrated surprise reflex, both fail.

## Related work

LeWorldModel [@maes2026lewm] trains the JEPA recipe we use end to end from pixels and plans with it in Push-T and in simulation; its planning uses fail here (chunk ranking, re-ranking). The identifiability analysis of LeJEPA [@klindt2026lejepa] predicts that SIGReg latents identify the state up to anisotropy, which holds here partially. DINO-WM [@zhou2024dinowm] plans on frozen DINOv2 [@oquab2023dinov2] patches, the design of our frozen control. V-JEPA 2.1 [@murlabadia2026vjepa21] and RoboJEPA [@zholus2026robojepa] are too slow on our on-board computer. ContactGuard [@zheng2026contactguard] finds that imagined-latent probes beat current-latent ones on grasp tasks; we find the opposite. ReAP [@liu2026reap] predicts action-conditioned risk, and its re-test misses its bar here, as do the hierarchical planners of HWM [@zhang2026hwm] and H-JEPA [@zhang2026hjepa] and the latent-distance scale of SURVEYOR [@surveyor2026]. Two results agree with ours from the control side: SaiVLA-JEPA [@xiao2026saivla] finds that a training-only future-latent head adds {{sai_gain}} points to control on {{sai_eps}} paired episodes, and HIJP [@hijp2026] finds LeWM below goal-conditioned behaviour cloning in simulation.

## Limitations

The controls are exploratory: {{n}} rollouts, operator labels, probes chosen after the data. Fresh rollouts scored under a bar committed first are the test, and until it runs every statement of the encoder/predictor split above is exploratory. One rig, one model, one data scale: a second scale point ({{scale_eps}} episodes) is costed and queued separately, and the scale curve belongs to a longer version. Not run: the surprise monitor and the imagined-latent grasp probe with the frozen DINOv2-S control or the joints (each needs its own predictor), and progress monotonicity with the controls (it needs a reimplementation). The bar of the outcome-readout use is retrospective, and the one same-commit bar is that of the two-level hierarchical progress use.

## Conclusion

On a small teleoperation dataset, a JEPA world model learns an encoder that reads how the episode went, a short horizon ahead included, and, in exploratory probes on the {{n}} rollouts, a predictor whose imagination adds nothing to that reading. Reporting every pre-registered use, not the best one, is what makes that visible.
